<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.3: Average Rate of Change of a Function</title>
<p>
    <alert>
        MTH 124
    </alert>
    </p>

    <definition workspace="0in">
        <title>Definition 1.3.4</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                For a function <m>f</m> defined on an interval <m>[a,b]</m>, the
                <term>average rate of change of <m>f</m> on <m>[a,b]</m></term>
                is the quantity
                <md>
                    AV_{[a,b]} = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p workspace="0.5in">
        Think about it: what is this related to if you have two points?
        (We will use this often! Take your own notes if you need to!)
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.3.2</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                According to the US census, the populations of Kent and Ottawa
                Counties in Michigan where GVSU is located from 1960 to 2010
                measured in 10-year intervals are given in the following tables.
            </p>
            <table>
              <title>Kent County population data.</title>
              <tabular>
                <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Year (<m>Y</m>)</cell>
                  <cell>1960</cell>
                  <cell>1970</cell>
                  <cell>1980</cell>
                  <cell>1990</cell>
                  <cell>2000</cell>
                  <cell>2010</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Population (<m>K(Y)</m>)</cell>
                  <cell>363,187</cell>
                  <cell>411,044</cell>
                  <cell>444,506</cell>
                  <cell>500,631</cell>
                  <cell>574,336</cell>
                  <cell>602,622</cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            </table>

                      <table>
              <title>Ottawa County population data.</title>
              <tabular>
                <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Year (<m>Y</m>)</cell>
                  <cell>1960</cell>
                  <cell>1970</cell>
                  <cell>1980</cell>
                  <cell>1990</cell>
                  <cell>2000</cell>
                  <cell>2010</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Population (<m>W(Y)</m>)</cell>
                  <cell>98,719</cell>
                  <cell>128,181</cell>
                  <cell>157,174</cell>
                  <cell>187,768</cell>
                  <cell>238,313</cell>
                  <cell>263,801</cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Let <m>K(Y)</m> represent the population of Kent County in year
                <m>Y</m> and <m>W(Y)</m> the population of Ottawa County in year
                <m>Y</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>AV_{[1990,2010]}</m> for both <m>K</m> and
                    <m>W</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
          <p>
            Kent: <m>AV_{[1990,2010]} = \frac{K(2010)-K(1990)}{2010-1990}=
            \frac{602,622 - 500,631}{20}=5099.55</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            Ottawa: <m>AV_{[1990,2010]} = \frac{W(2010)-W(1990)}{2010-1990}=
            \frac{263,801 - 187,768}{20}=3801.65</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What are the units on each of the quantities you computed in
                    (a)?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    people per year (output/input)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a careful sentence that explains the meaning of the
                    average rate of change of the Ottawa county population on
                    the time interval <m>[1990,2010]</m>.
                    Your sentence should begin something like “In an average
                    year between 1990 and 2010, the population of Ottawa County
                    was <m>\ldots</m> ”
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    Between 1990 and 2010, the population of Ottawa County
                    grew on average by 3802 people per year.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which county had a greater average rate of change during the
                    time interval <m>[2000,2010]</m>?
                    Were there any intervals in which one of the counties had a
                    negative average rate of change?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    During <m>[2000,2010]</m>, Kent county had 
                    a larger total growth, and thus a larger average rate of change over 
                    that time period. For a negative average rate of change, 
                    the population would need to decrease, which doesn't look like it 
                    happens in the table (at least for the years we are shown).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using the given data only, what do you predict was the
                    population of Ottawa County in 2018?
                    Why?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    In Ottawa, the latest change is 
                    <m>AV_{[2000,2010]}=\frac{W(2010)-W(2000)}{2010-2000}
                    =\frac{263,801 - 238,313}{10}=2548.8</m> people per year. 
                    In 2010 the population is 263,801. If we expect the population to go up by about 
                    2548.8 people per year, then 8 years later in 2018 we would 
                    expect the population to be <m>263,801 + 8(2548.8)\approx 284,191</m> people.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <definition workspace="0.15in">
        <title>Definition 1.3.7</title>

        <statement>
            <p workspace="1cm">
                Given a function <m>f</m>, we say it is
                <term>increasing on the interval <m>(a,b)</m></term> provided:
            </p>

            <p workspace="1cm">
                Similarly, we say that <m>f</m> is
                <term>decreasing on the interval <m>(a,b)</m></term> provided:
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.3.3. Function Trends</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let’s consider two different functions and see how different
                computations of their average rate of change tells us about
                their respective behavior.
                Plots of <m>q</m> and <m>h</m> are shown below.
                (Plot of <m>q</m> is on the left, <m>h</m> is on the right).
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
              <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            The parabola centered at (2,4) and opening down.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-1">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                              <definition> f(x)= 4 - (x-2)^2 </definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(2,1,13)" vticks="(-5,1,14)"  
                                    decorations="yes" hlabels="(2,2,12)" vlabels="(2,2,12)"/>
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(0,5)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,4.3)"><m>q(x)=4-(x-2)^2</m></label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            The parabola centered at (2,4) and opening down.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-2">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                              <definition> f(x)= 4 - (x-2)^2 </definition>
                              <definition> g(x)=3 - 2*(0.5)^x</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(2,1,13)" vticks="(-5,1,14)"  
                                    decorations="yes" hlabels="(2,2,12)" vlabels="(2,2,12)"/>
                                    <graph function="g"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,4.3)"><m>h(t)=3-2(0.5)^t</m></label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
            </sidebyside>

        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <m>q(x) = 4-(x-2)^2</m> shown in the
                    left graph.
                    Compute <m>AV_{[0,1]}</m>, <m>AV_{[1,2]}</m>,
                    <m>AV_{[2,3]}</m>, and <m>AV_{[3,4]}</m>.
                    What do your last two computations tell you about the
                    behavior of the function <m>q</m> on the interval
                    <m>[2,4]</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>AV_{[0,1]}=\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1-0}=\frac{3-0}{1}=3</m>
                    </p>
                <p> <m>AV_{[1,2]}=\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{2-1}=\frac{4-3}{1}=1</m>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                    <m>AV_{[2,3]}=\frac{q(3)-q(2)}{3-2}=\frac{3-4}{1}=-1</m> 
                    </p><p> <m>AV_{[3,4]}=\frac{q(4)-q(3)}{4-3}=\frac{0-3}{1}=-3</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The last two computations are negative, so the function/graph is decreasing on average.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <m>h(t) = 3-2(0.5)^t</m> shown in the
                    right graph.
                    Compute <m>AV_{[-1,1]}</m>, <m>AV_{[1,3]}</m>, and
                    <m>AV_{[3,5]}</m>.
                    What do your computations tell you about the behavior of the
                    function <m>h</m> on the interval <m>[-1,5]</m> ]?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>AV_{[-1,1]}=
                    \frac{h(1)-h(-1)}{1-(-1)}=
                    \frac{(3-2(0.5)^1) - (3-2(0.5)^{-1})}{2}=
                    \frac{2-(-1)}{2}=
                    1.5</m>
                </p>
                 <p>
                    <m>AV_{[1,3]}=
                    \frac{h(3)-h(1)}{3-1}=
                    \frac{(3-2(0.5)^3) - (3-2(0.5)^1)}{2}=
                    \frac{2.75-2}{2}=
                    0.375</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>AV_{[3,5]}=
                    \frac{h(5)-h(3)}{5-3}=
                    \frac{(3-2(0.5)^5) - (3-2(0.5)^3)}{2}=
                    \frac{2.94-2.75}{2}=
                    0.094</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The function is increasing on <m>[-1,5]</m> because
                    they are all positive, but it increases slower and slower...
                    (it increases at a decreasing rate!)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the graphs in the introduction to this activity, plot the line segments
                    whose respective slopes are the average rates of change you
                    computed in (a) and (b).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
              <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            The parabola centered at (2,4) and opening down.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-1-soln">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                              <definition> f(x)= 4 - (x-2)^2 </definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(2,1,13)" vticks="(-5,1,14)"  
                                    decorations="yes" hlabels="(2,2,12)" vlabels="(2,2,12)"/>
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(0,5)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,4.3)"><m>q(x)=4-(x-2)^2</m></label>
                                    <line p1="(0,f(0))" p2="(1,f(1))" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line p1="(1,f(1))" p2="(2,f(2))" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line p1="(2,f(2))" p2="(3,f(3))" dash="4 4"/>

                                    <line p1="(4,f(4))" p2="(3,f(3))" dash="4 4"/>

                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            The parabola centered at (2,4) and opening down.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-2-soln">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                              <definition> f(x)= 4 - (x-2)^2 </definition>
                              <definition> g(x)=3 - 2*(0.5)^x</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(2,1,13)" vticks="(-5,1,14)"  
                                    decorations="yes" hlabels="(2,2,12)" vlabels="(2,2,12)"/>
                                    <graph function="g"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,4.3)"><m>h(t)=3-2(0.5)^t</m></label>
                                    <line p1="(-1,g(-1))" p2="(1,g(1))" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line p1="(3,g(3))" p2="(1,g(1))" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line p1="(3,g(3))" p2="(5,g(5))" dash="4 4"/>

                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
            </sidebyside>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    True or false: Since <m>AV_{[0,3]} = 1</m>, the function
                    <m>q</m> is increasing on the interval <m>(0,3)</m>.
                    Justify your decision.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    Although it increases <em>on average</em>, 
                    it is not increasing everywhere, it actually 
                    decreases on <m>(2,3)</m>. Since the 
                    average rate of change is positive, it increases more than it 
                    decreases, but doesn't increase throughout the entire interval.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give an example of a function that has the same average rate
                    of change no matter what interval you choose.
                    You can provide your example through a table, a graph, or a
                    formula; regardless of your choice, write a sentence to
                    explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                <p>
                    Linear functions!  The slope is always constant!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.3.4</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Sketch at least two different possible graphs that satisfy the
                criteria for the function stated in each part.
                Make your graphs as significantly different as you can.
                If it is impossible for a graph to satisfy the criteria, explain
                why.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f</m> is a function defined on <m>[-1,7]</m> such that
                    <m>f(1) = 4</m> and <m>AV_{[1,3]} = -2</m>.
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                  <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A square grid between -2 and 8 to plot the function that meets the requirements (if possible).
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-3">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,16,16]">
                                    <grid-axes spacings="((-4,1,16),(-4,1,16))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(-4,2,16)" vticks="(-4,2,16)"  
                                    decorations="no"/>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A square grid between -2 and 8 to plot the function that meets the requirements (if possible).
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-3-4">
                            
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,16,16]">
                                    <grid-axes spacings="((-4,1,16),(-4,1,16))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(-4,2,16)" vticks="(-4,2,16)"  
                                    decorations="no"/>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </sidebyside>


            </statement>
            <solution component="aaa-soln">
                
                    <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
